dossier.distribution_plan:
Without the owner doing any work, customers can only come from store search, a capped ad budget, and slow web search traffic:

(1) App Store search optimization, run entirely by the AI. Apple states about 65% of downloads happen right after a search (secondary). The AI handles:
- keyword research from competitor listings and reviews;
- title and subtitle;
- screenshots generated with benefits first;
- about 10 localizations to widen keyword coverage;
- Product Page Optimization tests;
- replies to reviews through the App Store Connect API.

(2) A small Apple Search Ads learning budget, capped by the owner (for example CAD 15–30/day). The AI manages keywords and bids [Campaign Management API: prior knowledge, unverified, pre-2026-06]. Scale only if day-60 revenue per install exceeds the cost per install.

(3) An App Store featuring nomination. The AI drafts it, and a user with the Admin or Marketing role submits it; the recommended lead time is at least 3 weeks, per the perceva compilation.

(4) SEO landing pages and articles on Cloudflare Pages. This takes months to compound.

(5) Excluded by the owner's constraints: posting from the owner's personal social accounts, creator deals and community posting.

The evidence says these excluded channels drove the fast successes. Cal AI used 250+ creators on retainer, and Coconote used about 25 part-time creators. Hevy launched without an audience in 2019 and did not hit its inflection until January 2022. So this plan is honestly weak on distribution.

Owner hours/week: about 1–2 (approving spend and releases, relaying App Review messages if needed, reading the weekly report).
